# Suppresses a lock-order-inversion report that ThreadSanitizer raises
# against the fork-prepare handlers of this module and of cthreadcomm.
#
# Every module here that registers a pthread_atfork() prepare handler takes
# its own locks in that handler and holds them until after the fork, so the
# handlers' locks nest in whatever order the handlers run in. The reported
# cycle pairs clog_slot_table.rwlock, taken in _clog_atfork_prepare, with the
# queue registry mutex taken in _cthreadcomm_atfork_prepare, each one
# appearing to be acquired while the other is held.
#
# It can't deadlock. The C library runs prepare handlers one sequence at a
# time under its own atfork lock, so two handler sequences never interleave
# and neither can wait on a lock that the other holds. Inside one process
# the relative order of any two handlers is fixed as well, since it's the
# reverse of the registration order and the modules force their
# dependencies to register ahead of them. So the two directions of the
# cycle come from separate processes, and not from one lock-order graph that
# a single process could deadlock on. ThreadSanitizer's graph can't tell
# them apart, because a forked child inherits the parent's graph and keeps
# adding to it.
#
# We check the second half of that argument instead of just claiming it.
# Each prepare handler reports itself to _ccol_atfork_order_record (see
# _ccol_atfork_module_t in common.h), which records the pairwise order and
# aborts if two handlers ever run in opposite relative orders, and that is
# exactly the condition that would make this cycle real. The atfork_order
# tests of tests/common pin both directions of that check, so a real
# inversion stops the run even while this file keeps the detector quiet,
# which is what a suppression over our own code has to earn.
#
# The file silences output and does nothing else. The deadlock detector's
# reports don't set the exit status, so the ThreadSanitizer target passes
# with or without it; keeping it costs us those reports, and removing it
# costs a handful of lines of noise on every run of this suite.
#
# The entry is anchored on this module's own frame, which is always
# symbol-resolved, and not on a pthread-internal frame name, the same
# "anchor on our own code" choice that tests/chttpclient/tsan_openssl.supp
# makes. It names _clog_atfork_prepare because that's where the write lock
# is taken. An anchor naming a function that doesn't take the lock
# suppresses nothing, silently, and nothing fails until the reports show up
# somewhere else.
#
# Keep in mind what it hides. The anchor matches every lock-order-inversion
# report that carries that frame, including one between this module's
# prepare handler and another module's that didn't come from an inherited
# graph. _ccol_atfork_order_record exists to catch that class on its own,
# but whoever changes which locks a fork-prepare handler takes, or the order
# the handlers register in, should derive the reasoning above again and not
# trust a clean ThreadSanitizer run here.
deadlock:_clog_atfork_prepare

# A second, separate case: ThreadSanitizer doesn't model the
# re-initialization of a write-locked rwlock in a fork child.
#
# The fork-prepare handlers of this module and of cthreadcomm write-lock
# their process-wide slot-table rwlocks (clog_slot_table.rwlock and
# ccol_event_loop_slot_table.rwlock) on the thread that calls fork(). In the
# child that thread has a new thread ID, and the unlock of a glibc rwlock
# compares the recorded writer ID with the caller's, so a plain unlock would
# return 0 and release nothing. That's why the child handlers re-initialize
# both rwlocks. ThreadSanitizer keeps treating them as write-held by the
# child's main thread after the re-initialization, so a later write lock of
# either one in the child, by the exit drain or by a module destructor, is
# reported as "double lock of a mutex", and every lock the main thread takes
# after that is recorded as nested inside them, which gives the
# "lock-order-inversion" reports. Destroying the rwlock before the
# re-initialization only changes the report to "destroy of a locked mutex".
#
# It appears only in forked children of the two exit-drain tests that call
# exit() on the writer or compressor thread, or with the slot table held for
# ever, because only those children take the slot-table locks again after
# the fork. We check the behaviour these reports flag directly instead: the
# fork_safety tests of every module lock the same rwlocks in a child after a
# fork and fail with a hang if the child can't.
#
# Each anchor names the frame of our own code that takes the lock in the
# report, never a pthread or ThreadSanitizer frame, and the deadlock anchor
# names the test helper whose child produces the inversion, so an inversion
# anywhere else is reported as usual.
mutex:_clog_exit_drain
mutex:_cleanup_event_loop_slot_table
mutex:_clog_test_lock_table_forever
deadlock:exit_from_worker_child
